<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-finance-simple-interest" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Simple Interest and Discounts</title>

  <!-- This section is a remix of PCC, Sekhon and Bloom material, and my own original writing. -->

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Solve problems involving simple interest.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Solve problems involving discounted loans.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      It costs to borrow money.  The <q>rent</q> one pays for the use of money is called the <term>interest</term>.<idx>interest</idx> The amount of money that is being borrowed or loaned is called the <term>principal</term> or <term>present value</term>.<idx>principal</idx><idx>present value</idx>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-simple-interest">
    <title>Simple Interest</title>
    <p>
      Simple interest is interest paid only on the original amount borrowed.  In this scenario, the borrower pays a fixed interest rate on the principal for the entire time the money is held.
    </p>
    <p>
      Calculating interest starts with the principal, <m>P</m>, or the beginning amount in your account. This could be a starting investment or the starting amount of a loan. The <term>interest rate</term><idx>interest rate</idx>, <m>r</m>, in its most simple form, is a percentage of the principal.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, if you borrow $100 from a friend and agree to repay it with 5% interest, then the amount of interest you would pay would be 5% of $100. It is important to remember to change the interest rate, <m>r</m>, of 5% into a decimal by moving the decimal point two places to the left before performing any calculation.
    </p>
    <p>
      <md>(0.05)(\$100)=\$5</md>
    </p>
    <p>
      The total amount you would repay is called the <term>future value</term><idx>future value</idx> and would be $105, the original principal plus the interest.
    </p>
    <p>
      <md>\$100+\$5=\$105</md>
    </p>
    <p>
      One-time simple interest as in the above example is only common for extremely short-term or informal loans. For longer-term loans or investments, it is common for interest to be paid on a daily, monthly, quarterly, or annual basis. 
      In that case, interest would be earned regularly. 
    </p>
    <p>
      To see how this works, suppose you borrow $100 from a friend and agree to repay it with 5% interest per year, and you keep the money for 3 years. 
      How much would you need to repay at the end of the loan? The amount of interest you would pay would be 5% of $100 each year for three years, that is:    
    </p>
    <p>
      <md> (0.05)(\$100)(3) = \$15. </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      The future value of the loan would be $115, which is the original principal plus the interest.
    </p>
    <p>
      <md>\$100+\$15=\$115</md>
    </p>
    <p>
      These ideas are summarized in the block below.
    </p>
    <assemblage>
      <title>Simple Interest Formulas</title>
      <idx>simple interest</idx>
      <idx><h>financial formulas</h><h>simple interest</h></idx>
      <p>
        If an amount <m>P</m> is borrowed for a time <m>t</m> at an interest rate <m>r</m>, then the interest <m>I</m> is 
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>I=Prt.</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The amount <m>A</m> owed at the end of time <m>t</m> is given by
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>A =\amp P + I = P + Prt</mrow>
          <mrow> =\amp P(1+rt).</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Glossary:
      </p>
      <p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p><m>I</m> is the interest</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><m>P</m> is the principal, starting amount, or present value</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><m>r</m> is the interest rate in decimal form</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><m>t</m> is time, where the increment of time (years, months, etc.) matches the time period for the interest rate</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p><m>A</m> is the end amount, principal plus interest, or future value</p>
            </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
      Bonds are an example of a simple investment with a regular interest payout. Governments (federal, state, and local) and corporations issue bonds to raise money.
    </p>
    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose your city is building a new park, and issues bonds to raise the money to build it. You buy a $1,000 bond that pays 5% simple interest annually and matures in 5 years. How much interest will you earn? What is the future value of the bond?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Each year, you would earn 5% interest, so over the course of five years, you would earn:
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>\$1{,}000(0.05)(5)=\$250</md>
        </p>

        <p>
          When the bond matures, you would receive back the $1,000 you originally paid and the $250 in interest, so we could also put that into a single calculation:
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>\$1{,}000+\$1{,}000(0.05)(5)=\$1{,}250</md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <p>
      Here are a couple more examples.
    </p>
    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Ursula borrows $600 for 5 months at a simple interest rate of 15% per year.  Find the interest, and the total amount she is obligated to pay.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The interest is computed by multiplying the principal by the interest rate and the time.

          <md>
            I = \$600(0.15)\left(\frac{5}{12}\right) = \$37.50.
          </md>
          Note that here the rate is per year but the time is given in months, so we convert 5 months to years by dividing by 12.
        </p>
        <p>
          The total amount is thus
        </p>
        <p>
          <md> A = P + I = \$600 + \$37.50 = \$637.50.</md>
          This total can be computed directly using
          <md>
            <mrow> A =\amp P(1+rt) </mrow>
            <mrow> =\amp \$600[1+(0.15)(5/12)] </mrow>
            <mrow> =\amp \$600(1+0.0625)</mrow>
            <mrow> =\amp \$637.50. </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Darnel owes a total of $3,060, which includes 12% simple interest per year for the three years he borrowed the money.  How much did he originally borrow? 
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          This time we are asked to find the principal <m>P</m> given the future value.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$3{,}060 =\amp P[1+(0.12)(3)] </mrow>
            <mrow>\$3{,}060 =\amp P(1.36)</mrow>
            <mrow> \frac{\$3{,}060}{1.36} =\amp P </mrow>
            <mrow> \$2{,}250 =\amp P </mrow>
          </md>
          Darnel originally borrowed $2,250.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-simple-interest-discounts">
  <title>Discounts and Proceeds</title>
  <p>
    Lenders sometimes deduct the simple interest from the loan amount at the time that the loan is made.  When this happens, we say the loan has been <term>discounted</term>.  The interest that is deducted is called the <term>discount</term><idx><h>discounted loans</h><h>discount</h></idx>, and the actual amount that is given to the borrower is called the <term>proceeds</term>.<idx><h>discounted loans</h><h>proceeds</h></idx>  The amount the borrower is obligated to repay is called the <term>maturity value</term>.<idx><h>discounted loans</h><h>maturity value</h></idx>
  </p>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Discount and Proceeds Formulas</title>
    <idx>discounted loans</idx>
    <idx><h>financial formulas</h><h>discounted loans</h></idx>
    <p>
      If a loan with maturity value <m>L</m> is discounted for a time <m>t</m> at a simple interest rate <m>r</m>, then the discount <m>D</m> is 
    </p>
    <p>
      <md> D = Lrt. </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      The proceeds <m>R</m> are given by
    </p>
    <p>
      <md>
        <mrow>R =\amp L - D = L - Lrt</mrow>
        <mrow> =\amp L(1-rt).</mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Glossary:
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
          <li>
              <p><m>D</m> is the discount (interest)</p>
          </li>
          <li>
              <p><m>R</m> is the proceeds (the actual amount the borrower gets)</p>
          </li>
          <li>
              <p><m>r</m> is the interest rate in decimal form</p>
          </li>
          <li>
              <p><m>t</m> is time, where the increment of time (years, months, etc.) matches the time period for the interest rate</p>
          </li>
          <li>
              <p><m>L</m> is the amount of the loan that must be repaid, or the maturity value</p>
          </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Francisco borrows $1,200 from a bank for 10 months at a simple interest rate of 15% per year.  Determine the discount and the proceeds.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The discount <m>D</m> is the interest on the loan that the bank deducts from the loan amount.
        <md>
          <mrow>D =\amp Lrt </mrow>
          <mrow> =\amp \$1{,}200(0.15)\left(\frac{10}{12}\right) </mrow>
          <mrow> =\amp \$150 </mrow>
        </md>
        Therefore, the bank deducts $150 from the maturity value of $1,200 and gives Francisco $1,050. That is, <md> R = \$1{,}200 - \$150 = \$1{,}050.</md>  Francisco is obligated to repay the bank $1,200.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If Francisco wants to receive $1,200 for 10 months at a simple interest rate of 15% per year, what amount of loan should he apply for?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        In this problem, we are given the proceeds <m>R</m> and are being asked to find the maturity value <m>L</m>. From the problem statement we see that <m>R = \$1{,}200</m>, <m>r = 0.15</m>, and <m>t = 10/12</m>. We must solve for <m>L</m>. Thus
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>R =\amp L(1-rt) </mrow>
          <mrow>\$1{,}200 =\amp L\left[1-(0.15)\left(\frac{10}{12}\right)\right] </mrow>
          <mrow>\$1{,}200 =\amp L(1-0.125)</mrow>
          <mrow>\$1{,}200 =\amp L(0.875)</mrow>
          <mrow>L =\amp \frac{\$1{,}200}{0.875} </mrow>
          <mrow> \approx\amp \$1{,}371.43. </mrow>
        </md>
        Therefore, Francisco should ask for a loan of $1,371.43. The lender will discount the loan by $171.43 and Francisco will receive $1,200.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>
  </subsection>

  <exercises>
    <!-- Sources: Exercises 1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 11, 12, and 13 are adapted from
    Benjamin Jackson's MATH 105 lecture slides, clicker questions, and
    warm-up (Objective 1), Walla Walla University, Fall 2026. Exercises 7,
    8, and 14 are based on his MATH 105 written-homework question library
    (Fall 2026). Exercises 3, 9, and 10 are new.
    -->
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise><!--  #1 (Obj 1 clicker: 80% of 70) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                Write 6%, 4.5%, and 0.75% as decimals.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What is 35% of 80?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                Move the decimal point two places to the left: <m>6\% = 0.06</m>, <m>4.5\% = 0.045</m>, and <m>0.75\% = 0.0075</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>(0.35)(80) = 28</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #2 (Obj 1 slides: $1,000 at 3%) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          You borrow $1,000 at a simple interest rate of 3% per year. How much do you owe after 1 year? After 2 years? After 3 years? By how much does the amount you owe grow each year, and why is it the same every year?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Using <m>A = P(1+rt)</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>t = 1\colon \amp\quad A = \$1{,}000[1+(0.03)(1)] = \$1{,}030</mrow>
            <mrow>t = 2\colon \amp\quad A = \$1{,}000[1+(0.03)(2)] = \$1{,}060</mrow>
            <mrow>t = 3\colon \amp\quad A = \$1{,}000[1+(0.03)(3)] = \$1{,}090</mrow>
          </md>
          The amount owed grows by $30 each year. With simple interest, the interest is always computed on the original $1,000, so each year adds the same <m>(\$1{,}000)(0.03) = \$30</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #3 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          You invest $800 in an account that pays 4.5% simple interest per year. How much money is in the account after 4 years? How much of that is interest?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            A = \$800[1+(0.045)(4)] = \$800(1.18) = \$944
          </md>
          The account holds $944, of which <m>\$944 - \$800 = \$144</m> is interest.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #4 (Obj 1 slides: Hillary) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          A loan at a simple interest rate of 4.5% per year has grown to $10,000 after 8 years. How much was originally borrowed?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>\$10{,}000 =\amp P[1+(0.045)(8)]</mrow>
            <mrow>\$10{,}000 =\amp P(1.36)</mrow>
            <mrow>P =\amp \frac{\$10{,}000}{1.36} \approx \$7{,}352.94</mrow>
          </md>
          About $7,352.94 was originally borrowed.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #5 (Obj 1 slides: Randy; Obj 1 warm-up) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          You are $200 short on rent until your next paycheck. A lender offers to give you $200 today if you pay back $230 in two weeks.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                What annual simple interest rate is the lender charging? (Use 52 weeks in a year.)
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                A friend says, <q>That's only 15% interest. My credit card charges 24%, so this is a better deal.</q> Is your friend right? Explain.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                The interest is <m>\$230 - \$200 = \$30</m> and the time is <m>t = 2/52</m> year.
                <md>
                  <mrow>\$30 =\amp \$200 \cdot r \cdot \frac{2}{52}</mrow>
                  <mrow>r =\amp \frac{(30)(52)}{(200)(2)} = 3.9</mrow>
                </md>
                The annual rate is 390%.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                No. The 15% is charged for only two weeks, while the credit card's 24% is for a whole year. There are 26 two-week periods in a year, so the yearly rate is <m>26 \times 15\% = 390\%</m>, far more than 24%.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #6 (Obj 1 clicker: Jaqueline) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          A borrower is getting a loan at a simple interest rate of 6% per year. She plans to pay off the loan in 6 months and wants the total she pays back (principal plus interest) to be exactly $1,000. How much should she borrow?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>\$1{,}000 =\amp P[1+(0.06)(0.5)]</mrow>
            <mrow>\$1{,}000 =\amp P(1.03)</mrow>
            <mrow>P =\amp \frac{\$1{,}000}{1.03} \approx \$970.87</mrow>
          </md>
          She should borrow $970.87.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #7 (based on written 01a) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Mateo wants to buy a used e-bike for $1,800 in 15 months. How much should he invest now at 4% simple interest per year to have the money he needs then? How much interest will he earn?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Here <m>t = 15/12 = 1.25</m> years.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$1{,}800 =\amp P[1+(0.04)(1.25)]</mrow>
            <mrow>\$1{,}800 =\amp P(1.05)</mrow>
            <mrow>P =\amp \frac{\$1{,}800}{1.05} \approx \$1{,}714.29</mrow>
          </md>
          Mateo should invest $1,714.29 now. He will earn <m>\$1{,}800 - \$1{,}714.29 = \$85.71</m> in interest.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #8 (based on written 01b) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          A furniture store lets you finance a $2,400 sofa at a simple interest rate of 9% per year for 18 months, paid off in equal monthly payments.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                How much interest will you be charged?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What is the total amount you will repay?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What is the monthly payment?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                With <m>t = 18/12 = 1.5</m> years, <m>I = \$2{,}400(0.09)(1.5) = \$324</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\$2{,}400 + \$324 = \$2{,}724</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Eighteen equal payments are <m>\$2{,}724/18 \approx \$151.33</m> each.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #9 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          How many months will it take for $2,500 to grow to $2,575 at a simple interest rate of 3.6% per year?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The interest is <m>\$2{,}575 - \$2{,}500 = \$75</m>.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$75 =\amp \$2{,}500(0.036)t</mrow>
            <mrow>\$75 =\amp \$90t</mrow>
            <mrow>t =\amp \frac{75}{90} = \frac{5}{6} \text{ year}</mrow>
          </md>
          That is <m>\frac{5}{6}(12) = 10</m> months.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #10 (new) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          You pay $980 for a Treasury bill that pays you $1,000 in 26 weeks. What annual simple interest rate are you earning? Round to the nearest hundredth of a percent.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The interest is <m>\$1{,}000 - \$980 = \$20</m>, the principal is $980, and 26 weeks is <m>t = 0.5</m> year.
          <md>
            <mrow>\$20 =\amp \$980 \cdot r \cdot 0.5</mrow>
            <mrow>r =\amp \frac{20}{490} \approx 0.0408</mrow>
          </md>
          You are earning about 4.08% per year.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-finance-simple-discount-note"><!--  #11 (Obj 1 slides: $5,000 discounted note) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          A borrower signs a note for a discounted loan and agrees to pay the lender $5,000 in 18 months at a simple interest rate of 3% per year. What is the discount? How much does the borrower receive?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>D =\amp Lrt = \$5{,}000(0.03)(1.5) = \$225</mrow>
            <mrow>R =\amp L - D = \$5{,}000 - \$225 = \$4{,}775</mrow>
          </md>
          The discount is $225, and the borrower receives $4,775.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #12 (Obj 1 clicker: $4,775 loan; cheat-sheet closing point) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          This exercise continues <xref ref="exer-finance-simple-discount-note"/>.
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                Suppose instead you take an ordinary (not discounted) loan of $4,775 at a simple interest rate of 3% per year and pay it off in 18 months. How much interest do you pay?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Both loans put $4,775 in the borrower's hands for 18 months. Which one costs more in interest, and by how much?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What annual simple interest rate is the discounted loan really charging on the $4,775 the borrower receives? Round to the nearest hundredth of a percent.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>I = \$4{,}775(0.03)(1.5) = \$214.875 \approx \$214.88</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The discounted loan costs more: <m>\$225 - \$214.88 = \$10.12</m> more.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\$225 = \$4{,}775 \cdot r \cdot 1.5</m>, so <m>r = \frac{225}{7{,}162.5} \approx 0.0314</m>, or about 3.14%. The same advertised rate costs more when the interest is taken out first.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #13 (Obj 1 slides: Same Rate, Different Cost) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          Two lenders each charge 10% simple interest for one year. Lender A gives you $1,000, and you repay $1,100. Lender B takes the interest out first: you get $900 and repay $1,000. Both charge $100 in interest. What annual simple interest rate is Lender B really charging on the money you receive? Round to the nearest hundredth of a percent.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>\$100 = \$900 \cdot r \cdot 1</m>, so <m>r = \frac{100}{900} \approx 0.1111</m>. Lender B is really charging about 11.11%, compared with 10% for Lender A.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise><!--  #14 (based on written 01c) -->
      <statement>
        <p>
          A farmer needs $15,000 to buy an irrigation pump. The bank offers a discounted loan at a simple interest rate of 8% per year for 6 months. What loan amount (maturity value) should the farmer agree to repay so that the proceeds are $15,000? What is the discount?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>R =\amp L(1-rt)</mrow>
            <mrow>\$15{,}000 =\amp L[1-(0.08)(0.5)]</mrow>
            <mrow>\$15{,}000 =\amp L(0.96)</mrow>
            <mrow>L =\amp \frac{\$15{,}000}{0.96} = \$15{,}625</mrow>
          </md>
          The farmer should agree to repay $15,625. The discount is <m>\$15{,}625 - \$15{,}000 = \$625</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</section>